ge its title to 'Responses by code'. Then read the alert rule back and check whether it is enabled.\\n\\nWhen every step is done, end your reply with these two lines:\\nTIME_FIELD: <the data view's time field>\\nRULE_ENABLED: <yes or no, as you read it back>\"}]}]"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.time_to_first_chunk", "value": {"doubleValue": 26.961159}}], "status": {"code": 0}}, {"traceId": "0d0794cd710880254e93f718395571ec", "spanId": "e168a34de8157154", "parentSpanId": "90c7a2554b2d4f7f", "name": "chat qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "kind": 3, "startTimeUnixNano": "1791829371391753024", "endTimeUnixNano": "1791829384918953024", "attributes": [{"key": "gen_ai.conversation.id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T182221Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-4853f8"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.agent.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio server"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.provider.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.request.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.arm", "value": {"stringValue": "server"}}, {"key": "study.phase", "value": {"stringValue": "stage1"}}, {"key": "study.block", "value": {"stringValue": "b06"}}, {"key": "study.task", "value": {"stringValue": "weblogs"}}, {"key": "study.steps_total", "value": {"intValue": "4"}}, {"key": "study.run_id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T182221Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-4853f8"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.operation.name", "value": {"stringValue": "chat"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.finish_reasons", "value": {"arrayValue": {"values": [{"stringValue": "tool_call"}]}}}, {"key": "gen_ai.output.messages", "value": {"stringValue": "[{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"parts\": [{\"type\": \"reasoning\", \"content\": \"The data view has the following:\\n- Time field: `timestamp`\\n- Fields: response (string), response.keyword, bytes, request, etc.\\n\\nStep 1 complete. Time field is `timestamp`.\\n\\nStep 2: Create a dashboard titled \\\"LOC-cebf03e2 Web logs\\\" with exactly 2 panels:\\n1. Number of requests over time (date_histogram on timestamp, count)\\n2. Number of requests by HTTP response code (terms on response, count)\\n\\nFor panel 2, the response field is a string type. Group by response (terms). Should I use response.keyword for safety? Since response is a string type, Kibana typically has a .keyword sub-field for terms aggregations. Let's use response.keyword for the terms grouping. Actually, either should work, but response.keyword is the safe choice for a terms aggregation on a string field.\\n\\nLet's create the dashboard.\\n\"}, {\"type\": \"message\", \"content\": \"I've described the data view. Key findings:\\n- **Time field:** `timestamp`\\n- Relevant fields: `response` (string), `response.keyword`, `bytes`, `request`\\n\\nNow let me build the dashboard with the two panels.\\n\\n\"}, {\"type\": \"tool_call\", \"name\": \"create_dashboard\", \"arguments\": {\"title\": \"LOC-cebf03e2 Web logs\", \"panels\": [{\"title\": \"Requests over time\", \"chart_type\": \"line\", \"data_view\": \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\", \"metrics\": [{\"agg\": \"count\", \"label\": \"Requests\"}], \"group_by\": [{\"field\": \"timestamp\", \"kind\": \"date_histogram\"}]}, {\"title\": \"Requests by HTTP response code\", \"chart_type\": \"bar\", \"data_view\": \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\", \"metrics\": [{\"agg\": \"count\", \"label\": \"Requests\"}], \"group_by\": [{\"field\": \"response\", \"kind\": \"terms\", \"limit\": 10}]}]}}], \"finish_reason\": \"tool_call\"}]"}}, {"key": "agent.model.tool_calls", "value": {"intValue": "1"}}, {"key": "agent.model.thinking", "value": {"boolValue": true}}, {"key": "agent.model.finish_reason_inferred", "value": {"boolValue": true}}, {"key": "agent.secret.exposed", "value": {"boolValue": false}}], "status": {"code": 0}}, {"traceId": "0d0794cd710880254e93f718395571ec", "spanId": "20ef10b2817f0d1d", "parentSpanId": "90c7a2554b2d4f7f", "name": "chat qwen/qwen3.8-27b", "kind": 3, "startTimeUnixNano": "1791829391077853024", "endTimeUnixNano": "1791829425011953024", "attributes": [{"key": "gen_ai.conversation.id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T182221Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-4853f8"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.agent.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio server"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.provider.name", "value": {"stringValue": "lmstudio"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.request.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "study.arm", "value": {"stringValue": "server"}}, {"key": "study.phase", "value": {"stringValue": "stage1"}}, {"key": "study.block", "value": {"stringValue": "b06"}}, {"key": "study.task", "value": {"stringValue": "weblogs"}}, {"key": "study.steps_total", "value": {"intValue": "4"}}, {"key": "study.run_id", "value": {"stringValue": "20261012T182221Z-qwen-qwen3-8-27b-weblogs-4853f8"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.operation.name", "value": {"stringValue": "chat"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.model", "value": {"stringValue": "qwen/qwen3.8-27b"}}, {"key": "gen_ai.response.finish_reasons", "value": {"arrayValue": {"values": [{"stringValue": "tool_call"}]}}}, {"key": "gen_ai.output.messages", "value": {"stringValue": "[{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"parts\": [{\"type\": \"reasoning\", \"content\": \"Dashboard created with ID \\\"loc-cebf03e2-web-logs-9d906d0a8526\\\", panel count is 2.\\n\\nStep 3: Create an alert rule named \\\"LOC-cebf03e2 5xx errors\\\" against the web log data. Signal for the response code panel: check every minute and fire if more than 10 server errors occur within 5 minutes. Count all server errors: response code 500-599.\\n\\nThe response field is a string (\\\"response\\\" is a string type in the Kibana sample data, with values like \\\"200\\\", \\\"404\\\", \\\"503\\\"). So a .es-query rule with a range query on a string field doesn't work well. A range query on a string field doesn't work numerically... In fact, if the response field is indexed as a string (text/keyword), a range query would be lexicographic. In the Kibana sample data, \\\"response\\\" is a keyword field with values like \\\"200\\\", \\\"404\\\". A lexicographic range \\\"500\\\" to \\\"599\\\" on a keyword field: \\\"500\\\" <= value <= \\\"599\\\". For 3-digit codes, lexicographic comparison is the same as numeric comparison (all 3 digits). Since all response codes are 3 digits, \\\"500\\\" <= x <= \\\"599\\\" works lexicographically: for example \\\"500\\\" <= \\\"503\\\" <= \\\"599\\\" ✓. And \\\"200\\\" is out of range ✓. Therefore a range query against keyword works.\\n\\nAs an alternative, use match queries against each code from 500 to 599? That would be 100 match clauses. The range query is cleaner: {\\\"range\\\": {\\\"response\\\": {\\\"gte\\\": \\\"500\\\", \\\"lte\\\": \\\"599\\\"}}}.\\n\\nLet me construct the .es-query parameters:\\n- searchType: \\\"esQuery\\\"\\n- esQuery: JSON string: \\\"{\\\\\\\"query\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"range\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"response\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"gte\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"500\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"lte\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"599\\\\\\\"}}}}\\\"\\n- index: [\\\"kibana_sample_data_logs\\\"]\\n- timeField: \\\"timestamp\\\"\\n- threshold: [10]\\n- thresholdComparator: \\\">\\\"\\n- timeWindowSize: 5\\n- timeWindowUnit: \\\"m\\\"\\n- size: 100\\n\\n\\\"Fire when more than 10 server errors occur\\\" → threshold [10] and comparator \\\">\\\" means count > 10. Yes.\\n\\nSchedule interval: \\\"1m\\\" (every minute).\\n\\nAfter that, enable it. First let me create it (it'll be created in a disabled state), then enable it.\\n\"}, {\"type\": \"message\", \"content\": \"The dashboard was created with 2 panels. Next is step 3: create an alert rule. The `response` field is a string/keyword type (values like \\\"200\\\", \\\"503\\\"), so I'll use a range query on that field to cover codes from 500 to 599 (which is valid as lexicographic ordering for 3-digit codes).\\n\\n\"}, {\"